WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.190
Two liquids.

00:00:02.190 --> 00:00:04.430
Either one by itself,

00:00:04.430 --> 00:00:06.285
but mix them...

00:00:07.642 --> 00:00:08.475
[explosion booms]

00:00:08.475 --> 00:00:09.923
Lloyd Davies: I'd like
to say I've never been to

00:00:09.923 --> 00:00:11.940
a police station where
someone picked up an IED

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:13.567
and brought it into the police station,

00:00:13.567 --> 00:00:14.700
but unfortunately I have,

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:16.330
so that's pretty realistic.

00:00:16.330 --> 00:00:17.613
My name's Lloyd Davies.

00:00:17.613 --> 00:00:20.995
I'm a former British Army
ammunition technical officer.

00:00:20.995 --> 00:00:23.770
Through my time in
military, I specialized in

00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:26.030
counterterrorism, hostage
rescue, bomb disposal,

00:00:26.030 --> 00:00:28.450
or improvised-explosive-device disposal.

00:00:28.450 --> 00:00:30.430
I'm now a defense consultant,

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:31.880
and I also work with industry

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:34.560
to develop counter-EOD capability.

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:36.760
And today I'll be reviewing

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:38.893
some Hollywood bomb-disposal scenes.

00:00:42.247 --> 00:00:45.540
Sonny: Once old fat-head
Shaw got on that plane.

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:47.710
Trent: Felt something
depress under my right foot.

00:00:47.710 --> 00:00:49.207
Pretty sure it's a land mine.

00:00:49.207 --> 00:00:50.995
Lloyd: His reaction in
this particular case

00:00:50.995 --> 00:00:53.180
is very realistic of the kind of guy.

00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:56.060
You know, these are SEALs.

00:00:56.060 --> 00:00:58.833
They are incredibly
professional, incredibly stoic.

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:01.361
Jason: Nobody move.

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:03.580
We're in a minefield.

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:05.870
Lloyd: What we saw there,
that pile of rocks,

00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:08.640
is fairly realistic,
particularly in Afghanistan,

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:11.860
where pressure plate IEDs
were placed quite a lot.

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:14.080
So that's a little pressure
plate buried in the ground

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:17.010
attached to explosives to
target personnel or vehicles.

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:18.490
They would often be marked,

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:21.450
to either warn the local population,

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:23.487
and they would mark them
with piles of stones,

00:01:23.487 --> 00:01:25.270
rags, flags, that kind of thing.

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:27.950
The issue I have is that's
not how land mines work.

00:01:27.950 --> 00:01:31.340
There is absolutely no benefit
in designing a land mine

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:34.060
that works on arming under pressure

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:35.860
and then firing on pressure release.

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:39.360
What you're trying to do
is initiate the explosion

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:43.140
when that target is in the
exact place you want it to be.

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:45.550
And in this case, the exact
place you want that target to be

00:01:45.550 --> 00:01:48.217
when the mine functions
is directly on top of it.

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:53.320
And it's a huge misnomer
that there's this opportunity

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:56.890
to stand on a land mine,
stay perfectly still,

00:01:56.890 --> 00:01:59.150
and then work your way
out of that situation.

00:01:59.150 --> 00:02:01.850
If you stand on it, that's
it, it's gonna function.

00:02:01.850 --> 00:02:03.440
Were he in this situation,

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:05.910
with his foot over a pressure plate,

00:02:05.910 --> 00:02:08.478
I think maybe they are going
about this the right way.

00:02:08.478 --> 00:02:09.860
They're probably limited very much

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:11.060
in what their options are.

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:12.760
So, what it looks like
they're trying to do is

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:14.590
they've created two fixed
points in the ground.

00:02:14.590 --> 00:02:15.920
They're gonna tie a string between them

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:18.510
to keep the tension down
over that pressure plate.

00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:20.200
How they act, how they respond and stuff,

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:22.080
yeah, I can believe that.

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:24.212
But unfortunately from an
explosive point of view,

00:02:24.212 --> 00:02:25.970
that's a zero.

00:02:27.070 --> 00:02:29.010
[plastic rustles]

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:31.028
OK, right now he's just
diving straight in there,

00:02:31.028 --> 00:02:34.703
which is probably not the best approach.

00:02:34.703 --> 00:02:37.760
Yeah, yeah, of course you'd
stroke it, that's fine.

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:39.538
When the British Army
were working in Iraq,

00:02:39.538 --> 00:02:40.500
towards the back end,

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:43.830
the bomb suit itself
was becoming tailored.

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:46.740
We would only use the top
half of the bomb suit,

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:49.300
mainly because of the heat and exhaustion.

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:50.483
You're gonna be doing this for about

00:02:50.483 --> 00:02:53.330
45 minutes to an hour, in ridiculous heat,

00:02:53.330 --> 00:02:56.713
so the bomb suit's realistic, that's fine.

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:01.225
Approaching without
using your robot first,

00:03:01.225 --> 00:03:02.420
probably less so.

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:04.380
Really, you wanna do minimal
interaction with the bomb.

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:07.340
So very rarely would you
just start picking at things,

00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:08.985
moving stuff around.

00:03:08.985 --> 00:03:11.735
[James grunts]

00:03:13.610 --> 00:03:16.145
He's identified the detonating cord

00:03:16.145 --> 00:03:17.861
that's going into the main charge itself,

00:03:17.861 --> 00:03:19.090
which is an artillery shell.

00:03:19.090 --> 00:03:21.530
And he's trying to
separate that detonator,

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:23.550
what we call breaking
down the explosive train.

00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:25.710
So, it's not completely rendering it safe,

00:03:25.710 --> 00:03:28.280
but it's gonna take that small pop,

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:29.870
which is still pretty dangerous,

00:03:29.870 --> 00:03:30.930
but it's not gonna bring down

00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:31.970
the entire of that neighborhood.

00:03:31.970 --> 00:03:35.160
So, fairly reasonable sort of RSP,

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:36.877
or render safe procedure, so far.

00:03:39.694 --> 00:03:41.032
James: Gotcha.

00:03:41.032 --> 00:03:42.907
Lloyd: So, he's just
pulling that detonator,

00:03:42.907 --> 00:03:44.840
or blasting cap,

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:46.489
out of the fuse well.

00:03:46.489 --> 00:03:48.240
Absolutely no looking around

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:49.481
to see what else it's connected to

00:03:49.481 --> 00:03:51.660
or what that wire might be doing.

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:53.720
James: All right, we're done.

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:54.870
Good to go.

00:03:55.723 --> 00:03:57.720
Lloyd: There are lots of
guys watching, recording,

00:03:57.720 --> 00:03:59.500
and filming what he's doing.

00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:00.824
That's absolutely realistic.

00:04:00.824 --> 00:04:02.490
The bomb placer is most
likely going to be watching

00:04:02.490 --> 00:04:04.590
so they understand what we do.

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:06.093
James: Uh, got a wire.

00:04:07.940 --> 00:04:09.010
Lloyd: Yeah. We don't do that either.

00:04:09.010 --> 00:04:11.694
We don't pull at wires
to see where they go.

00:04:11.694 --> 00:04:14.186
[tense music]
[gravel scraping]

00:04:17.110 --> 00:04:21.017
So, this is actually really
common in Afghanistan,

00:04:21.017 --> 00:04:22.654
and particularly in Iraq.

00:04:22.654 --> 00:04:24.300
They'd do what we call daisy chains.

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:26.950
So they'd link multiple main charges,

00:04:26.950 --> 00:04:28.750
usually along the side of the road.

00:04:28.750 --> 00:04:29.630
And what he's doing there is

00:04:29.630 --> 00:04:31.360
he's pulling on the electric wires

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:32.990
that are connected to the blasting cap

00:04:32.990 --> 00:04:36.100
that previously he'd just
removed by hand quite easily.

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:37.308
Now, those shells are gonna be

00:04:37.308 --> 00:04:40.748
somewhere in the region
of 25 kilos upwards.

00:04:40.748 --> 00:04:42.740
He's now just dragging around

00:04:42.740 --> 00:04:44.848
and pulling it out of the dirt.

00:04:44.848 --> 00:04:46.513
Not something you want to be doing.

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:50.320
We're gonna see him run straight to

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:51.745
what we call the firing point,

00:04:51.745 --> 00:04:55.535
which is the two bare wires on the wall.

00:04:57.530 --> 00:05:00.630
[James pants]

00:05:00.630 --> 00:05:03.030
If this is what we call a proxy bomb,

00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:04.337
they'll probably have a handler.

00:05:04.337 --> 00:05:05.888
They'll probably have someone watching,

00:05:05.888 --> 00:05:08.205
and there'll be two triggers
to this particular bomb.

00:05:08.205 --> 00:05:10.860
There'll be the intended trigger,

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:13.460
so a push button or
something on the vest itself,

00:05:13.460 --> 00:05:15.600
something that they're carrying
or maybe strapped into,

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:18.870
but there are most likely,
particularly in Iraq

00:05:18.870 --> 00:05:20.680
the mobile-phone threat was pretty high,

00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:23.173
so there'd be probably what we call an RC,

00:05:23.173 --> 00:05:26.523
or a remote-control trigger
attached to the vest as well,

00:05:26.523 --> 00:05:28.680
for precisely this situation.

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:29.950
And we'd call that a chicken switch.

00:05:29.950 --> 00:05:32.909
So if the bomber decided to chicken out,

00:05:32.909 --> 00:05:35.956
his handler could detonate
him at a moment's notice.

00:05:35.956 --> 00:05:37.830
[gasping]

00:05:37.830 --> 00:05:38.939
James: Oh, man.

00:05:38.939 --> 00:05:41.850
Lloyd: The priority is separating
the bomb from the person,

00:05:41.850 --> 00:05:43.370
not necessarily dealing with the bomb.

00:05:43.370 --> 00:05:46.300
So he'd be gone as soon as
that's been dropped off.

00:05:46.300 --> 00:05:48.518
James: I can't get it off. I'm sorry, OK?

00:05:48.518 --> 00:05:50.120
You understand? I'm sorry.

00:05:50.120 --> 00:05:51.260
I'm sorry!
[man sobs]

00:05:51.260 --> 00:05:52.414
Get down!

00:05:52.414 --> 00:05:54.060
Lloyd: As an EOD operator,
you feel particularly

00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:56.890
responsible, and there
is very little he can do.

00:05:56.890 --> 00:06:00.241
There's no point in wasting his own life.

00:06:00.241 --> 00:06:01.835
[man prays]

00:06:01.835 --> 00:06:04.668
[explosion roars]

00:06:05.910 --> 00:06:09.238
It's one of the more
realistic Hollywood explosions

00:06:09.238 --> 00:06:10.820
that I'd say we've seen.

00:06:10.820 --> 00:06:13.585
There was a sizeable
amount of plastic explosive

00:06:13.585 --> 00:06:14.640
all over that guy.

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:18.660
If you can double the distance
between you and the device,

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:20.040
you actually expose yourself

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:22.466
to about a quarter of the shock wave

00:06:22.466 --> 00:06:24.210
and blast wave that you experience.

00:06:24.210 --> 00:06:27.507
So, any foot he could have
got further away from that

00:06:27.507 --> 00:06:29.555
would've helped.

00:06:29.555 --> 00:06:32.390
So, the first scene we
saw, for absolute realism,

00:06:32.390 --> 00:06:34.967
I'd give it about a
two or three out of 10.

00:06:34.967 --> 00:06:36.776
For this scene, probably a seven.

00:06:36.776 --> 00:06:38.586
Benji: Right, stand by.

00:06:38.586 --> 00:06:40.348
[dramatic music]

00:06:40.348 --> 00:06:41.640
Three.

00:06:41.640 --> 00:06:43.027
Two.

00:06:43.027 --> 00:06:44.574
One
Luther: Now!

00:06:44.574 --> 00:06:46.680
Lloyd: I remember watching
this in the cinema,

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:49.310
and I happened to be there with
some colleagues at the time,

00:06:49.310 --> 00:06:51.330
one of whom was actually tasked

00:06:51.330 --> 00:06:53.810
with being part of the
team that would render safe

00:06:53.810 --> 00:06:55.610
improvised nuclear devices,

00:06:55.610 --> 00:06:57.810
and some of the others were
special forces operators.

00:06:57.810 --> 00:07:00.350
And we all had a lot
to say about this clip.

00:07:00.350 --> 00:07:02.160
The bomb itself is quite reminiscent

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:04.010
of the bomb in "Goldfinger."

00:07:04.010 --> 00:07:06.400
There's a really weird mix of

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:08.990
mechanical devices clicking
around and going on

00:07:08.990 --> 00:07:10.360
and complicated electronics.

00:07:10.360 --> 00:07:12.240
You're particularly never
going to get two timers,

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:15.100
one mechanical, one digital
counting down at the same time.

00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:17.463
Luther: Turn the screw counterclockwise.

00:07:17.463 --> 00:07:18.296
Benji: Got it.

00:07:18.296 --> 00:07:20.458
Luther: When the time
comes, cut the green wire.

00:07:20.458 --> 00:07:21.770
Do not cut it yet.

00:07:21.770 --> 00:07:22.910
Benji: OK, we copy.

00:07:22.910 --> 00:07:24.551
Lloyd: Another thing is this

00:07:24.551 --> 00:07:26.934
innate knowledge of the heroes,

00:07:26.934 --> 00:07:29.760
to have a almost
textbook-like understanding

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:31.717
of the makeup of these
devices and be able to say,

00:07:31.717 --> 00:07:33.650
"Oh, it's this switch from this area.

00:07:33.650 --> 00:07:34.483
We're going to do this."

00:07:34.483 --> 00:07:35.640
That doesn't happen.

00:07:35.640 --> 00:07:36.680
It's bespoke.

00:07:36.680 --> 00:07:37.713
There are certain things,

00:07:37.713 --> 00:07:40.260
the generic makeup of
how these things operate,

00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:43.161
you can't get around physics,

00:07:43.161 --> 00:07:47.073
but knowing exactly what
color wire and things to do,

00:07:47.073 --> 00:07:49.567
it's just not believable.
It's not realistic.

00:07:49.567 --> 00:07:52.215
[tense music]

00:07:52.215 --> 00:07:53.705
[metal dings]

00:07:53.705 --> 00:07:55.267
[Lloyd laughs]

00:07:55.267 --> 00:07:57.730
And on cutting that wire
he's built in the software

00:07:57.730 --> 00:08:00.840
to just slowly open up and
expose the core of this device.

00:08:00.840 --> 00:08:03.306
There's absolutely no
reason why the bomb designer

00:08:03.306 --> 00:08:05.610
would design that feature into this bomb.

00:08:05.610 --> 00:08:07.028
Even if you've managed to render safe

00:08:07.028 --> 00:08:09.590
the trigger mechanism, all of this, right,

00:08:09.590 --> 00:08:11.300
there's still a huge amount of work to do

00:08:11.300 --> 00:08:13.150
to start taking that apart and get rid of

00:08:13.150 --> 00:08:15.730
the actual radioactive element to this.

00:08:15.730 --> 00:08:17.020
You've got multiple devices

00:08:17.020 --> 00:08:19.553
all having to be cut at
exactly the same time.

00:08:19.553 --> 00:08:21.262
You know, they're all synchronized.

00:08:21.262 --> 00:08:23.100
That just doesn't work.

00:08:23.100 --> 00:08:27.590
We've tried cutting wires
at simultaneous times

00:08:27.590 --> 00:08:30.370
with multiple people across
different parts of the room,

00:08:30.370 --> 00:08:33.570
or even on the same device,
if everything's linked.

00:08:33.570 --> 00:08:35.510
We've tried it in training.

00:08:35.510 --> 00:08:37.272
There is just no way you're
going to be fast enough

00:08:37.272 --> 00:08:39.660
to beat switches in an electrical system

00:08:39.660 --> 00:08:41.600
by cutting things simultaneously.

00:08:41.600 --> 00:08:43.220
I'll give it a two out of 10,

00:08:43.220 --> 00:08:45.635
just because the physics.

00:08:45.635 --> 00:08:47.477
Charlie: We found this in a playground.

00:08:47.477 --> 00:08:50.560
This stuff is cutting
edge. It's a binary liquid.

00:08:50.560 --> 00:08:51.400
Walter: A what?

00:08:51.400 --> 00:08:52.790
Lloyd: Yeah, that's pretty common

00:08:52.790 --> 00:08:55.903
for an EOD tech to completely
geek out and get excited

00:08:55.903 --> 00:08:57.769
about the device he's found

00:08:57.769 --> 00:08:58.994
or that he's been doing.

00:08:58.994 --> 00:09:02.090
I'd like to say I've never
been to a police station

00:09:02.090 --> 00:09:04.410
where someone's picked up an IED

00:09:04.410 --> 00:09:06.180
and brought it into the police station,

00:09:06.180 --> 00:09:09.690
but unfortunately I have,
so that's pretty realistic.

00:09:09.690 --> 00:09:11.710
Charlie: Two liquids.

00:09:11.710 --> 00:09:13.963
Either one by itself,

00:09:13.963 --> 00:09:15.811
but mix them...

00:09:17.120 --> 00:09:18.203
[tense music]

00:09:18.203 --> 00:09:19.036
[explosion booms]

00:09:19.036 --> 00:09:20.600
Lloyd: So, yeah, binary explosives exist.

00:09:20.600 --> 00:09:23.020
Well, essentially, any
simple explosive mixture

00:09:23.020 --> 00:09:26.710
is two separate chemicals
just mixed together.

00:09:26.710 --> 00:09:28.502
In this case, he's mixing

00:09:28.502 --> 00:09:30.080
what would be a fuel and an oxidizer

00:09:30.080 --> 00:09:32.510
to make an incredibly sensitive explosive,

00:09:32.510 --> 00:09:33.640
very high-tech bomb,

00:09:33.640 --> 00:09:35.570
very impressive explosive technology.

00:09:35.570 --> 00:09:36.798
And you're definitely not gonna get

00:09:36.798 --> 00:09:39.080
that kind of explosion
out of that small amount.

00:09:39.080 --> 00:09:42.114
This guy being a serial bomb maker,

00:09:42.114 --> 00:09:43.420
they have existed in the past,

00:09:43.420 --> 00:09:45.040
they probably will exist again.

00:09:45.040 --> 00:09:47.470
In a large bombing campaign like that,

00:09:47.470 --> 00:09:49.640
even Afghanistan, Iraq,
when we were there,

00:09:49.640 --> 00:09:52.260
the bomb maker will develop a signature.

00:09:52.260 --> 00:09:54.070
They will develop multiple bombs.

00:09:54.070 --> 00:09:55.220
And a lot of what we did

00:09:55.220 --> 00:09:58.062
in particularly Afghanistan
when we were serving there

00:09:58.062 --> 00:10:00.310
was try and gather evidence,

00:10:00.310 --> 00:10:03.870
be it forensics or just bomb style.

00:10:03.870 --> 00:10:05.210
So the technology exists.

00:10:05.210 --> 00:10:08.406
So I'd give it a six out of 10.

00:10:08.406 --> 00:10:10.760
Bomb tech: Then try to dive into the tub.

00:10:10.760 --> 00:10:11.816
Roger: I, I can't do it.

00:10:11.816 --> 00:10:12.858
Martin: He can't do it, man. His legs,

00:10:12.858 --> 00:10:15.130
he's been on there for,
like, 18, 20 hours.

00:10:15.130 --> 00:10:17.180
He can't even walk, let
alone hop off the can.

00:10:17.180 --> 00:10:19.484
Lloyd: OK, so that's actually
a really, really good point.

00:10:19.484 --> 00:10:20.680
His legs are probably numb,

00:10:20.680 --> 00:10:24.365
he's fatigued, he's
dehydrated, he's scared.

00:10:24.365 --> 00:10:26.190
He's unlikely to be jumping up

00:10:26.190 --> 00:10:28.180
and running around anywhere anytime soon.

00:10:28.180 --> 00:10:31.176
He might, however, just faint
and fall off the toilet,

00:10:31.176 --> 00:10:33.560
so I'd probably be
physically restraining him

00:10:33.560 --> 00:10:35.490
and holding him in place
at this point in time.

00:10:37.330 --> 00:10:38.860
Bomb tech: Now, what he's doing, sergeant,

00:10:38.860 --> 00:10:41.080
is spraying this thing
with liquid nitrogen.

00:10:41.080 --> 00:10:43.950
That should give you a good
second or two before detonation.

00:10:43.950 --> 00:10:45.851
Lloyd: Covering something
in liquid nitrogen

00:10:45.851 --> 00:10:48.352
to freeze the switch in place,

00:10:48.352 --> 00:10:50.210
in theory it's a mechanical attack;

00:10:50.210 --> 00:10:53.020
you're attacking the physical
mechanisms of that switch

00:10:53.020 --> 00:10:54.390
and holding it in place.

00:10:54.390 --> 00:10:56.200
It's going to do nothing
for the electrics.

00:10:56.200 --> 00:10:57.570
It's not a good idea.

00:10:57.570 --> 00:10:58.970
It would not be something we'd do

00:10:58.970 --> 00:11:02.060
against an improvised
explosive device like this.

00:11:02.060 --> 00:11:04.202
You have no idea what that extreme cold

00:11:04.202 --> 00:11:05.630
temperature's going to do,

00:11:05.630 --> 00:11:07.740
how the components are
going to react to that.

00:11:07.740 --> 00:11:09.230
They're pouring it in the wrong place.

00:11:09.230 --> 00:11:11.005
The switch is gonna be
right underneath him.

00:11:11.005 --> 00:11:13.127
Bomb tech: OK, get them up, get them out.

00:11:13.127 --> 00:11:15.117
Let's go. Move, move.

00:11:15.958 --> 00:11:17.460
Lloyd: First of all, there's
only gonna be one person

00:11:17.460 --> 00:11:18.560
dealing with this.

00:11:19.690 --> 00:11:21.270
Bomb tech: All right, let's move.

00:11:21.270 --> 00:11:24.150
Lloyd: Yep, bomb blankets,
containment of explosions,

00:11:24.150 --> 00:11:25.893
all things we try and do.

00:11:25.893 --> 00:11:28.726
[explosion roars]

00:11:29.731 --> 00:11:32.108
[toilet thuds]

00:11:32.108 --> 00:11:33.881
That is one sturdy toilet.

00:11:33.881 --> 00:11:35.489
The whole side of the house goes out,

00:11:35.489 --> 00:11:37.720
but that porcelain toilet stays intact.

00:11:37.720 --> 00:11:39.420
He's probably better off
just sitting on top of that

00:11:39.420 --> 00:11:41.077
rather than jumping in the tub.

00:11:41.077 --> 00:11:42.200
You know, at the very minimum,

00:11:42.200 --> 00:11:44.000
you'd expect his eardrums to rupture.

00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:45.880
All the soft tissues in his body,

00:11:45.880 --> 00:11:47.060
particularly his lungs and his bowel,

00:11:47.060 --> 00:11:49.980
are very susceptible to
blast wave, shock wave.

00:11:49.980 --> 00:11:51.700
Essentially, you get a really bad bruise

00:11:51.700 --> 00:11:54.346
all over those linings.
There's lots blood.

00:11:54.346 --> 00:11:56.630
And you can suffer from what
we call dry-land drowning,

00:11:56.630 --> 00:11:58.330
which is where all the blood vessels

00:11:58.330 --> 00:12:00.260
in the lining of your lung rupture

00:12:00.260 --> 00:12:02.201
and your lungs are filled with blood,

00:12:02.201 --> 00:12:03.620
essentially, over time.

00:12:03.620 --> 00:12:07.293
Or you get blast bowel, which
is slightly less severe,

00:12:07.293 --> 00:12:08.440
but essentially it's the same thing,

00:12:08.440 --> 00:12:09.810
your bowel starts filling with blood

00:12:09.810 --> 00:12:11.018
and you bleed internally.

00:12:11.018 --> 00:12:12.980
In training I've dealt
with a toilet bomb before.

00:12:12.980 --> 00:12:16.547
The probably weirdest place I've found one

00:12:16.547 --> 00:12:18.560
was in a bathroom, originally.

00:12:18.560 --> 00:12:20.530
By the time I'd gotten to it,

00:12:20.530 --> 00:12:22.560
the landlord had thrown it out the window,

00:12:22.560 --> 00:12:23.527
it'd been collected by the police,

00:12:23.527 --> 00:12:24.847
and driven back to the police station.

00:12:24.847 --> 00:12:26.272
I'd give it one out of 10.

00:12:27.264 --> 00:12:29.300
Fallon: I'm going to cut the blue wire.

00:12:30.679 --> 00:12:32.281
And my lads will wait.

00:12:33.165 --> 00:12:35.460
If I go in the wrong way,

00:12:35.460 --> 00:12:37.190
they'll know what to do.

00:12:37.190 --> 00:12:39.203
Lloyd: So, there's so much
about this that I really like.

00:12:39.203 --> 00:12:41.190
So, I think his attitude and demeanor,

00:12:41.190 --> 00:12:43.130
doing what he's doing, is perfect.

00:12:43.130 --> 00:12:44.410
The communication back and forth.

00:12:44.410 --> 00:12:46.320
So that reach back, as we call it,

00:12:46.320 --> 00:12:49.200
to someone who's not under that pressure,

00:12:49.200 --> 00:12:51.570
someone who's got the scientific support

00:12:51.570 --> 00:12:52.680
and support of other people around them,

00:12:52.680 --> 00:12:56.371
can think maybe slightly
clearer or cooler than you are,

00:12:56.371 --> 00:12:57.703
is very realistic.

00:12:57.703 --> 00:12:59.445
Sidney: Cut the blue wire.

00:12:59.445 --> 00:13:01.250
[tense music]

00:13:01.250 --> 00:13:04.450
Lloyd: It's never gonna be
a simple red or blue choice.

00:13:04.450 --> 00:13:07.320
A bomb is gonna contain
at least seven components,

00:13:07.320 --> 00:13:09.530
from a power source to
your main explosives

00:13:09.530 --> 00:13:11.280
to your initiator switches.

00:13:11.280 --> 00:13:13.970
It's never as simple,
unfortunately, as red or blue.

00:13:13.970 --> 00:13:16.561
And if it is, you do a
little bit more investigating

00:13:16.561 --> 00:13:18.345
before you decide to cut a wire.

00:13:18.345 --> 00:13:22.422
[tense music]
[bomb beeping]

00:13:22.422 --> 00:13:23.255
[wire snaps]

00:13:23.255 --> 00:13:25.170
And now there's one guiding principle

00:13:25.170 --> 00:13:27.620
when we're working this kind of job,

00:13:27.620 --> 00:13:29.880
and if you say you're gonna
do something, you do it,

00:13:29.880 --> 00:13:31.220
for exactly that reason.

00:13:31.220 --> 00:13:32.550
If you couldn't explain it to your mother

00:13:32.550 --> 00:13:33.820
as to why you're taking that action,

00:13:33.820 --> 00:13:35.530
you shouldn't be doing it.

00:13:35.530 --> 00:13:37.390
So you are 100% sure

00:13:37.390 --> 00:13:39.350
when you do anything as an EOD operator.

00:13:39.350 --> 00:13:43.595
No way that you swap between decisions

00:13:43.595 --> 00:13:45.650
or not do something that
you said you were gonna do.

00:13:45.650 --> 00:13:49.450
So, the vast majority of
IEDs that we've experienced,

00:13:49.450 --> 00:13:51.876
in Afghanistan particularly,
are very, very simple.

00:13:51.876 --> 00:13:53.840
They're very simple,
very simple electronics,

00:13:53.840 --> 00:13:55.150
very simple circuits.

00:13:55.150 --> 00:13:57.900
If you were to cut any of the wires,

00:13:57.900 --> 00:13:59.880
you've probably broken the bomb enough

00:13:59.880 --> 00:14:01.718
to make it somewhat safer.

00:14:01.718 --> 00:14:03.170
Without having done a full investigation

00:14:03.170 --> 00:14:04.664
of what you're dealing with,

00:14:04.664 --> 00:14:06.530
you generally don't start cutting wires

00:14:06.530 --> 00:14:08.005
and hoping or guessing at things.

00:14:08.005 --> 00:14:09.870
That's a very surefire way to make sure

00:14:09.870 --> 00:14:12.960
that you don't survive
very long doing this job.

00:14:12.960 --> 00:14:15.350
He would never've changed
his mind at that last moment.

00:14:15.350 --> 00:14:17.103
So, unfortunately, I can't
give it 10 out of 10,

00:14:17.103 --> 00:14:20.003
but I'm going to go with a
pretty high nine on this one.

00:14:24.260 --> 00:14:25.837
Officer: I've laid out what you'll need.

00:14:25.837 --> 00:14:28.045
Lloyd: Great, so you you've got the

00:14:28.045 --> 00:14:30.778
external explosives officer from SO15,

00:14:30.778 --> 00:14:32.624
counterterrorism police.

00:14:32.624 --> 00:14:35.540
They're a unit that
exists. They're very good.

00:14:35.540 --> 00:14:38.460
They are all ex-British
Army bomb-disposal operators

00:14:38.460 --> 00:14:40.151
who transferred over to work

00:14:40.151 --> 00:14:41.530
for the police and with the police.

00:14:41.530 --> 00:14:45.170
And he's gonna let the
hostage diffuse his own bomb,

00:14:45.170 --> 00:14:48.110
which is probably his first mistake.

00:14:48.110 --> 00:14:50.080
Could easily have put that
small tool bag on the robot

00:14:50.080 --> 00:14:51.685
to send that down to the hostage.

00:14:51.685 --> 00:14:54.040
David: I'm not gonna manage with one hand.

00:14:54.040 --> 00:14:56.120
Officer: Well, start by
freeing your left hand.

00:14:56.120 --> 00:14:58.530
Lloyd: He's holding
down what they call DMS.

00:14:58.530 --> 00:15:00.219
It's not an abbreviation we use.

00:15:00.219 --> 00:15:03.210
In this case it stands
for dead man's switch.

00:15:03.210 --> 00:15:06.590
Whilst that button may
be very light to push,

00:15:06.590 --> 00:15:08.870
you press a button for an hour

00:15:08.870 --> 00:15:10.870
and you're gonna feel some
extreme pain in your hand.

00:15:10.870 --> 00:15:13.320
So, he has taped his thumb
down onto it already,

00:15:13.320 --> 00:15:14.780
which is something we would do.

00:15:14.780 --> 00:15:17.950
Quite lucky actually, that
bomb is pretty poorly designed.

00:15:17.950 --> 00:15:19.350
All the circuits exposed.

00:15:19.350 --> 00:15:20.650
That's a pretty generous bomb maker,

00:15:20.650 --> 00:15:22.769
not to just put a cover over all of that.

00:15:22.769 --> 00:15:25.020
That decision between,
is this a suicide bomber

00:15:25.020 --> 00:15:25.910
or is this a hostage?

00:15:25.910 --> 00:15:27.628
Maybe if you're entirely unsure

00:15:27.628 --> 00:15:30.930
and you're suspecting more
on the suicide-bomber lines,

00:15:30.930 --> 00:15:32.710
then, yeah, maybe send a
robot with some tools down

00:15:32.710 --> 00:15:35.890
and try to get them to start
doing something to help out.

00:15:35.890 --> 00:15:37.830
In this case, it would
probably be much more

00:15:37.830 --> 00:15:40.700
exposing the device,
showing it to the robot,

00:15:40.700 --> 00:15:41.720
showing the components.

00:15:41.720 --> 00:15:43.654
If there's other
switches, as I was saying,

00:15:43.654 --> 00:15:45.980
mobile phones, radios, that
kind of stuff on there,

00:15:45.980 --> 00:15:49.130
you wouldn't necessarily
start getting the hostage

00:15:49.130 --> 00:15:51.563
to start taking the device apart,

00:15:51.563 --> 00:15:53.043
as you're about to see now.

00:15:53.043 --> 00:15:55.060
Officer: Slip the spacer under your thumb,

00:15:55.060 --> 00:15:57.403
being sure to maintain
pressure on the DMS trigger.

00:16:01.225 --> 00:16:02.560
Lloyd: That particular action so far

00:16:02.560 --> 00:16:05.440
is what we call mechanical blocking.

00:16:05.440 --> 00:16:08.231
So you're basically doing a

00:16:08.231 --> 00:16:10.681
very risky physical procedure.

00:16:10.681 --> 00:16:12.950
You're not interacting
with anything electrically.

00:16:12.950 --> 00:16:14.603
All he's doing is replacing his thumb

00:16:14.603 --> 00:16:16.853
with something to hold
that button down in place.

00:16:16.853 --> 00:16:18.790
Before we leave what we call the ICP,

00:16:18.790 --> 00:16:21.420
the incident control point,
and make that manual approach,

00:16:21.420 --> 00:16:24.240
or in this case start
doing any manual actions,

00:16:24.240 --> 00:16:27.979
we have a 100% plan of
what we're going to do.

00:16:28.929 --> 00:16:30.429
[David moans]

00:16:33.601 --> 00:16:35.590
Yeah, no one's letting go of that switch.

00:16:35.590 --> 00:16:36.815
No one is confident enough

00:16:36.815 --> 00:16:38.157
that they're gonna lift
their thumb off that

00:16:38.157 --> 00:16:40.820
without an EOD operator
making them move it.

00:16:40.820 --> 00:16:42.490
There's a whole load of
other things you'd get to

00:16:42.490 --> 00:16:43.800
before you went anywhere near that.

00:16:43.800 --> 00:16:45.810
And the last thing I'd do

00:16:45.810 --> 00:16:47.650
is trust the tape that's been on his hand

00:16:47.650 --> 00:16:49.567
that's covered in mud, blood, and sweat.

00:16:49.567 --> 00:16:52.067
[tense music]

00:16:54.930 --> 00:16:56.410
OK, so this is where this scene

00:16:56.410 --> 00:16:58.180
goes even more wrong for me.

00:16:58.180 --> 00:16:59.671
Without any diagnostics

00:16:59.671 --> 00:17:01.104
or understanding what you're doing,

00:17:01.104 --> 00:17:05.575
trying to just jab electric
wires into a circuit board

00:17:05.575 --> 00:17:08.000
is a really bad thing to do with a bomb.

00:17:08.000 --> 00:17:11.800
This whole clock speeding up and things

00:17:11.800 --> 00:17:13.191
doesn't happen.

00:17:13.191 --> 00:17:15.810
You might do something, and
the best you can hope for

00:17:15.810 --> 00:17:16.790
is nothing happening.

00:17:16.790 --> 00:17:18.830
So I winced at this the
first time I watched it,

00:17:18.830 --> 00:17:20.000
and I had a lot of people texting me,

00:17:20.000 --> 00:17:21.207
asking me how real it was.

00:17:21.207 --> 00:17:23.743
So I'm gonna give it two out of 10.

00:17:23.743 --> 00:17:25.830
Stanley: Agent Stanley Goodspeed,

00:17:25.830 --> 00:17:27.550
agent trainee Marvin Isherwood,

00:17:27.550 --> 00:17:30.149
initiating exam of wooden crate.

00:17:30.149 --> 00:17:31.485
Lloyd: Hazmat suits, they're a thing.

00:17:31.485 --> 00:17:33.631
We use them in certain situations.

00:17:33.631 --> 00:17:35.410
Hazmat suits are designed specifically

00:17:35.410 --> 00:17:37.327
for these chemical agents.

00:17:37.327 --> 00:17:39.345
Marvin: Hi, there, Stanley!
Stanley: Marvin, don't!

00:17:39.345 --> 00:17:41.430
[gas hisses]
S--t.

00:17:41.430 --> 00:17:43.210
Lloyd: That's kudos to the
bomb maker right there.

00:17:43.210 --> 00:17:46.370
I mean, to make a creepy baby
doll that sprays sarin gas.

00:17:46.370 --> 00:17:48.395
This is not a learning opportunity.

00:17:48.395 --> 00:17:50.745
All your training is done
in a training environment

00:17:50.745 --> 00:17:52.470
where there's no danger.

00:17:52.470 --> 00:17:54.063
Stanley: There's enough C-4 explosive

00:17:54.063 --> 00:17:55.940
and poison gas to blow the whole chamber

00:17:55.940 --> 00:17:57.550
and kill everybody in the building.

00:17:57.550 --> 00:17:58.610
Lloyd: It doesn't make sense that he's got

00:17:58.610 --> 00:18:00.630
two main charges, or two payloads.

00:18:00.630 --> 00:18:05.630
You've got the sarin gas that's
a chemical warfare agent,

00:18:05.633 --> 00:18:07.390
that's a neurotoxin.

00:18:07.390 --> 00:18:10.920
And what that does is
it paralyzes the muscles

00:18:10.920 --> 00:18:13.550
by blocking what's called ACh.

00:18:13.550 --> 00:18:17.120
And that's the chemical
messaging system between nerves.

00:18:17.120 --> 00:18:18.680
Totally deadly on its own.

00:18:18.680 --> 00:18:20.380
There is absolutely no need

00:18:20.380 --> 00:18:23.618
to put enough C-4 in
there to blow up the room.

00:18:23.618 --> 00:18:25.370
Lonner: Tell them to
take the atropine, now.

00:18:25.370 --> 00:18:27.120
Man: Marvin, the atropine, now!

00:18:27.120 --> 00:18:30.300
Lonner: Inject it in your
heart before your suit melts!

00:18:30.300 --> 00:18:31.900
Lloyd: Well, atropine's a real thing.

00:18:32.973 --> 00:18:35.831
Atropine is the somewhat

00:18:35.831 --> 00:18:38.990
de facto antidote to nerve agent.

00:18:38.990 --> 00:18:41.210
You don't have to inject it
directly into your heart.

00:18:41.210 --> 00:18:44.980
You generally go for a
large, fleshy muscle area

00:18:44.980 --> 00:18:46.443
like your thigh or buttocks.

00:18:47.304 --> 00:18:49.246
[bomb beeps]
Stanley: 15!

00:18:49.246 --> 00:18:51.010
Lloyd: Great, we're now soaking

00:18:51.010 --> 00:18:53.760
an electrically initiated IED with water,

00:18:53.760 --> 00:18:55.100
generally a really bad idea.

00:18:55.100 --> 00:18:57.490
You have no idea what short
circuit that's gonna create

00:18:57.490 --> 00:18:59.730
or what damage that's gonna do.

00:18:59.730 --> 00:19:02.150
[metal grates]

00:19:02.150 --> 00:19:04.370
Stanley: Really elegant
string-of-pearls configuration.

00:19:04.370 --> 00:19:06.680
Unfortunately, incredibly unstable.

00:19:06.680 --> 00:19:07.847
Lloyd: Yeah. So,

00:19:07.847 --> 00:19:12.090
an incredibly unstable,
elegant string of pearls

00:19:12.090 --> 00:19:14.390
is a terrible weapon design.

00:19:14.390 --> 00:19:16.890
So, that sits within a rocket

00:19:16.890 --> 00:19:19.003
that's gonna get launched
by a booster motor

00:19:19.003 --> 00:19:20.660
and is gonna fly over the speed of sound,

00:19:20.660 --> 00:19:23.310
or however fast this thing is gonna go.

00:19:23.310 --> 00:19:24.845
Unfortunately, that's gonna be subject

00:19:24.845 --> 00:19:27.330
to a lot of physics,
and one of those forces

00:19:27.330 --> 00:19:29.990
it's gonna be subject to is setback force,

00:19:29.990 --> 00:19:33.360
where essentially the
acceleration of the rocket

00:19:33.360 --> 00:19:37.300
increases the g-force of those pearls,

00:19:37.300 --> 00:19:39.270
and they're gonna get
compressed against each other

00:19:39.270 --> 00:19:40.130
really, really hard.

00:19:40.130 --> 00:19:42.770
So pretty much instantly as
soon as that thing launches,

00:19:42.770 --> 00:19:43.700
those are all gonna crack,

00:19:43.700 --> 00:19:45.840
and you're just gonna get melted VX

00:19:45.840 --> 00:19:47.842
spraying out of this thing
as it's flying along.

00:19:47.842 --> 00:19:49.580
Stanley: It's a cholinesterase inhibitor.

00:19:49.580 --> 00:19:51.240
Stops the brain from
sending nerve messages

00:19:51.240 --> 00:19:53.430
down the spinal cord within 30 seconds.

00:19:53.430 --> 00:19:56.108
But that's after your skin melts off.

00:19:56.108 --> 00:19:59.025
Lloyd: That's fairly accurate
with how you feel it.

00:19:59.960 --> 00:20:01.268
What you wouldn't get is the huge

00:20:01.268 --> 00:20:03.007
blistering and skin melting

00:20:03.007 --> 00:20:04.805
that you saw earlier in the movie.

00:20:04.805 --> 00:20:08.110
Fantastic movie, unfortunately
not very realistic at all.

00:20:08.110 --> 00:20:12.043
Not from an EOD or
weapons-design point of view.

00:20:12.043 --> 00:20:14.621
Jimmy: Disk drive sounds funny.
Something's slowing it down.

00:20:14.621 --> 00:20:16.845
Nancy: Yeah, I could've told
you that. What're we gonna do?

00:20:16.845 --> 00:20:18.770
Lloyd: It would take probably
more effort than it's worth

00:20:18.770 --> 00:20:23.200
to set that up, as far
as programming goes.

00:20:23.200 --> 00:20:25.741
If she's been sat there for
hours typing that code out,

00:20:25.741 --> 00:20:27.600
I'd probably get the guy in the bomb suit

00:20:27.600 --> 00:20:28.891
to start typing the code for her,

00:20:28.891 --> 00:20:31.620
I'd give her a bit of
a rest, let her relax.

00:20:31.620 --> 00:20:33.500
Jimmy: Hi.
Nancy: Who the hell are you?

00:20:33.500 --> 00:20:34.633
Jimmy: I'm Dove.

00:20:35.750 --> 00:20:37.066
James Dove.

00:20:38.400 --> 00:20:39.980
Lloyd: Typically in a
hostage situation like this

00:20:39.980 --> 00:20:42.179
it is possible,

00:20:42.179 --> 00:20:43.579
and one of the key things we try and do

00:20:43.579 --> 00:20:45.279
is build rapport.

00:20:45.279 --> 00:20:47.080
You've gotta try and keep
them as calm as you can.

00:20:47.080 --> 00:20:50.635
Yeah, you would strip down,

00:20:50.635 --> 00:20:52.220
or remove the barriers as far as helmets

00:20:52.220 --> 00:20:53.290
and body armor and the likes,

00:20:53.290 --> 00:20:54.820
to get on with this.

00:20:54.820 --> 00:20:56.451
Jimmy: You like red wine or white wine?

00:20:56.451 --> 00:20:58.532
Nancy: I don't give a
s--t, just do something!

00:20:58.532 --> 00:21:00.290
Jimmy: Red, me too.

00:21:00.290 --> 00:21:03.240
Lloyd: The truth is, we
don't guess at anything.

00:21:03.240 --> 00:21:04.430
If we don't know, we don't do it.

00:21:04.430 --> 00:21:07.949
So I'd probably go for a
two out of 10 for this one.

00:21:07.949 --> 00:21:09.360
Ned: I don't know, maybe I'm wrong.

00:21:09.360 --> 00:21:10.835
You guys are the experts,

00:21:10.835 --> 00:21:12.358
maybe you don't need me.

00:21:12.358 --> 00:21:14.183
This is, um,

00:21:15.508 --> 00:21:17.624
this is Semtex, am I right?

00:21:17.624 --> 00:21:18.620
Plastique.

00:21:18.620 --> 00:21:22.070
Lloyd: OK, so he's the disgruntled,

00:21:22.070 --> 00:21:23.653
unappreciated specialist.

00:21:23.653 --> 00:21:25.270
So far, he's identified

00:21:25.270 --> 00:21:27.620
some kind of plastic explosive as Semtex.

00:21:27.620 --> 00:21:29.470
I'm not convinced at this moment in time,

00:21:29.470 --> 00:21:31.628
but, you know, have I seen

00:21:31.628 --> 00:21:33.745
stuff laid out on a police table

00:21:33.745 --> 00:21:35.750
or in a police lockup that
probably shouldn't be there

00:21:35.750 --> 00:21:37.450
and probably should have been dealt with?

00:21:37.450 --> 00:21:38.670
I absolutely have.

00:21:38.670 --> 00:21:39.880
Ned: What else do we have here?

00:21:39.880 --> 00:21:41.638
Oh, this is a, um,

00:21:41.638 --> 00:21:42.729
a plunger detonator, am I right?

00:21:42.729 --> 00:21:44.590
And that's a little mini receiver.

00:21:44.590 --> 00:21:47.630
Lloyd: He's talking absolute
rubbish at this point.

00:21:47.630 --> 00:21:49.505
He's waffling about

00:21:49.505 --> 00:21:52.138
plunger detonators and whatever else.

00:21:52.138 --> 00:21:53.270
Utter nonsense.

00:21:53.270 --> 00:21:56.501
So, for technical accuracy,

00:21:56.501 --> 00:21:58.873
that's basically a zero.

